window.MCA301_INDEX=[{"title":"Data Communication","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson01.html","text":" Data Communication Learning objectives: Define data communication, identify its components, explain data representation and distinguish data-flow modes. Definition and Purpose Data communication is the exchange of data between two or more devices through a transmission medium using agreed communication rules (protocols). A basic communication system contains a sender, receiver, message, transmission medium and protocol. 0.88 Components Message: information to be communicated. Sender: device that originates the message. Receiver: device that accepts the message. Medium: physical or wireless path. Protocol: rules governing communication. Data Representation Text is represented using character"},{"title":"Networks: Distributed Processing, Criteria, Physical Structure and Categories","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson02.html","text":" Networks: Distributed Processing, Criteria, Physical Structure and Categories Learning objectives: Explain distributed processing, network criteria, physical structures and common network categories. Distributed Processing In distributed processing, computational tasks and resources are distributed among multiple interconnected systems rather than being performed by one central computer. Communication networks enable resource sharing and coordination. Network Criteria Important criteria include performance, reliability and security. Performance can be considered using throughput and delay; reliability includes failure frequency and recovery; security concerns protection against unauthorized"},{"title":"Network Topologies","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson03.html","text":" Network Topologies Learning objectives: Explain bus, star, ring, mesh and tree topologies with advantages and limitations. 0.92 Bus All devices share a common backbone. It can be economical but a backbone fault can affect communication and shared-medium contention must be managed. Star Each node connects to a central device. It is easy to manage and isolate a single edge-link fault, but failure of the central device can affect many nodes. Ring Each node has a logical/physical ring relationship. Ring implementations differ in access control and fault behavior. Mesh Nodes have many interconnections. Full mesh offers multiple paths but requires many links; partial mesh reduces cost while retai"},{"title":"OSI Reference Model and TCP/IP Model","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson04.html","text":" OSI Reference Model and TCP/IP Model Learning objectives: Explain the OSI layers, responsibilities and relationship to the TCP/IP model. 0.55 OSI Layers Application -- network services to applications. Presentation -- representation, translation, compression and related functions. Session -- session establishment/management concepts. Transport -- process-to-process delivery and transport services. Network -- logical addressing and routing. Data Link -- framing, link control and error detection/correction functions. Physical -- transmission of bits/signals. TCP/IP Model The Internet architecture is commonly described using application, transport, Internet/network, and link/network-access fun"},{"title":"Physical Layer: Data and Signals","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson05.html","text":" Physical Layer: Data and Signals Learning objectives: Distinguish analog/digital data and signals and explain basic signal parameters. Data and Signals Data is information; a signal is the physical/electromagnetic representation used to carry that information. Analog data varies continuously; digital data is represented using discrete values. Analog signals vary continuously; digital signals use discrete signal levels/waveforms. Basic Signal Concepts Amplitude describes magnitude; frequency is cycles per second (Hz); period is $T=1/f$; phase describes relative timing/position of a periodic waveform. Bit Rate and Baud Rate Bit rate is bits per second. Baud rate is signal elements per second."},{"title":"Transmission Impairment and Performance","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson06.html","text":" Transmission Impairment and Performance Learning objectives: Explain attenuation, distortion, noise, bandwidth, throughput, propagation delay and transmission delay. Transmission Impairments Attenuation: loss of signal strength. Distortion: change in signal shape because frequency components are affected differently. Noise: unwanted energy that alters the received signal. Performance Measures Bandwidth is a frequency range or, in a digital context, may refer to capacity depending on context. Throughput is the actual achieved data rate. Propagation delay is time for the signal to travel through the medium: d_{prop}=\\frac{distance}{propagation speed}. \\] Transmission delay is time to place al"},{"title":"Multiplexing, Transmission Media and Switching","unit":"Unit 1","url":"units/unit1/lesson07.html","text":" Multiplexing, Transmission Media and Switching Learning objectives: Explain FDM, WDM, TDM, guided/unguided media and circuit/packet switching. 0.92 Multiplexing Multiplexing allows multiple signals/flows to share a common medium. FDM: users occupy different frequency bands simultaneously. WDM: optical wavelengths carry separate channels over fiber. TDM: users share the channel at different time slots. Transmission Media Guided: twisted-pair cable, coaxial cable and fiber-optic cable. Unguided: radio waves, microwaves and infrared. Switching 0.90 Circuit switching establishes a dedicated path/resources for a communication session. Packet switching divides data into packets that share network"},{"title":"Data Link Layer: Framing and Flow Control","unit":"Unit 2","url":"units/unit2/lesson01.html","text":" Data Link Layer: Framing and Flow Control Learning objectives: Explain framing, flow control and the basic responsibilities of the data link layer. 0.9 Data Link Layer The data link layer provides node-to-node delivery over a link and commonly handles framing, link addressing, error detection and flow/access control depending on the technology. Framing Framing divides a bit stream into identifiable frames. Common approaches include character/byte-oriented framing, byte stuffing, bit stuffing and physical-layer coding violations in suitable systems. Flow Control Flow control coordinates sender and receiver so the sender does not overwhelm the receiver. Stop-and-wait sends one frame and waits"},{"title":"Error Detection and Correction: Parity, Checksum, CRC and Hamming Code","unit":"Unit 2","url":"units/unit2/lesson02.html","text":" Error Detection and Correction: Parity, Checksum, CRC and Hamming Code Learning objectives: Solve parity/checksum/CRC/Hamming examples and distinguish detection from correction. 0.92 Parity A parity bit is added so the number of 1s is even or odd. It is simple but has limited detection capability. Example Data = 1011001 has four 1s. Even parity bit = 0; odd parity bit = 1. Checksum Words are added using one's-complement arithmetic and the final complement is transmitted. Receiver verification is performed using the corresponding one's-complement rule. CRC CRC treats the bit string as a polynomial over GF(2). The sender appends zeros, divides by the generator polynomial using modulo-2 divisi"},{"title":"Data Link Protocols: Stop-and-Wait, Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat ARQ","unit":"Unit 2","url":"units/unit2/lesson03.html","text":" Data Link Protocols: Stop-and-Wait, Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat ARQ Learning objectives: Explain ARQ operation, ACK/timeout behavior and compare Stop-and-Wait, Go-Back-N and Selective Repeat. 0.9 ARQ Automatic Repeat reQuest uses acknowledgments, sequence numbers and retransmission to recover from lost/corrupted frames. Stop-and-Wait ARQ The sender transmits one frame and waits for an ACK. On timeout or negative indication it retransmits. Sequence numbering is used to distinguish duplicates. Go-Back-N The sender may transmit multiple frames. The receiver generally uses cumulative acknowledgments and may discard out-of-order frames; after a loss, the sender retransmits the missing frame a"},{"title":"Wireless LANs: IEEE 802.11 and Bluetooth","unit":"Unit 2","url":"units/unit2/lesson04.html","text":" Wireless LANs: IEEE 802.11 and Bluetooth Learning objectives: Describe the syllabus-level concepts of IEEE 802.11 wireless LANs and Bluetooth. 0.85 IEEE 802.11 IEEE 802.11 specifies wireless LAN technologies. At the syllabus level, important concepts include wireless stations, access points, association, shared-medium access and frame-based communication. CSMA/CA is used rather than Ethernet-style collision detection because a wireless station generally cannot reliably detect a collision while transmitting. Bluetooth Bluetooth is a short-range wireless technology family used for personal-area communication and device connectivity. It is designed for low-power, short-range links and supports"},{"title":"Virtual-Circuit Networks: Frame Relay and ATM","unit":"Unit 2","url":"units/unit2/lesson05.html","text":" Virtual-Circuit Networks: Frame Relay and ATM Learning objectives: Explain virtual-circuit concepts and outline Frame Relay and ATM. 0.9 Virtual Circuit A virtual circuit is a logical path established through a packet-switched network. Packets/frames belonging to the connection follow the logical circuit and use an identifier rather than carrying a complete destination decision at every hop in the same manner as a pure datagram service. Frame Relay Frame Relay is a WAN technology using virtual circuits and frame switching, historically associated with efficient digital links and relatively low protocol overhead. ATM Asynchronous Transfer Mode uses fixed-size 53-byte cells: 5-byte header and"},{"title":"Logical Addressing: IPv4 and IPv6 Addresses","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson01.html","text":" Logical Addressing: IPv4 and IPv6 Addresses Learning objectives: Explain logical addressing, IPv4 structure, IPv6 structure and basic notation. 0.9 Logical Addressing Network-layer logical addresses identify interfaces/nodes for internetwork delivery. IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses; IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses. IPv4 IPv4 addresses are written in dotted-decimal notation, e.g. 192.0.2.10. Prefix notation such as /24 identifies the network-prefix length. IPv6 IPv6 addresses are 128 bits and commonly written in hexadecimal groups separated by colons. Leading zeros within a group may be suppressed; one contiguous run of zero groups may be compressed using ::. Addressing Concepts Network prefix, inte"},{"title":"Internet Protocol: IPv4 and IPv6","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson02.html","text":" Internet Protocol: IPv4 and IPv6 Learning objectives: Explain the network-layer forwarding role of IPv4/IPv6 and major differences. IP Function IP provides best-effort network-layer datagram delivery across interconnected networks. Routers forward packets according to destination address and routing information. IPv4 IPv4 includes a variable-length header with fields such as source/destination addresses, TTL, protocol and checksum. Routers can fragment IPv4 packets under the IPv4 rules, although modern networks often use path-MTU discovery to avoid fragmentation. IPv6 IPv6 has a fixed 40-byte base header and extension headers. It replaces TTL with Hop Limit and uses Next Header to identify "},{"title":"Transition from IPv4 to IPv6: Dual Stack and Tunneling","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson03.html","text":" Transition from IPv4 to IPv6: Dual Stack and Tunneling Learning objectives: Explain why transition mechanisms are needed and describe dual stack and tunneling. Need for Transition IPv4 and IPv6 are different network protocols. During migration, networks may need mechanisms allowing IPv4 and IPv6 systems to coexist. Dual Stack A dual-stack host/network supports both IPv4 and IPv6 and selects the appropriate protocol for communication. Tunneling Tunneling carries one protocol inside another across a portion of the path. For example, IPv6 packets can be encapsulated inside IPv4 to cross an IPv4-only segment, subject to the particular tunnel mechanism. 2-Mark Answer Dual stack means supporting "},{"title":"Address Mapping: ARP, RARP and DHCP","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson04.html","text":" Address Mapping: ARP, RARP and DHCP Learning objectives: Explain address mapping/configuration functions of ARP, legacy RARP and DHCP. 0.88 ARP Address Resolution Protocol maps an IPv4 protocol address to a link-layer address on the local network. A host may broadcast an ARP request and the target can reply with its link-layer address. RARP Reverse ARP historically allowed a host to obtain an IPv4 address using a link-layer address. It has been superseded by more capable configuration mechanisms such as BOOTP and DHCP. DHCP DHCP dynamically supplies configuration information such as an IP address, subnet parameters and gateway/DNS information according to the deployment. 0.9 2-Mark Answer A"},{"title":"Network Layer Protocols: ICMP and IGMP","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson05.html","text":" Network Layer Protocols: ICMP and IGMP Learning objectives: Explain the purposes of ICMP and IGMP and distinguish control messaging from data forwarding. 0.8 ICMP Internet Control Message Protocol supports control, diagnostic and error-reporting messages associated with IP. Examples include destination-unreachable and time-exceeded messages; ping uses ICMP Echo messages. IGMP Internet Group Management Protocol supports IPv4 multicast group membership management between hosts and local multicast routers. 2-Mark Answer ICMP provides IP control/diagnostic messaging; IGMP manages IPv4 multicast group membership. 5-Mark Answer Explain ICMP and IGMP with examples. 10-Mark Answer Discuss the purpo"},{"title":"Routing: Unicast, RIP, OSPF, Distance Vector and Link State","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson06.html","text":" Routing: Unicast, RIP, OSPF, Distance Vector and Link State Learning objectives: Explain routing, unicast forwarding, distance-vector and link-state concepts, RIP and OSPF. 0.9 Routing Routing determines paths through an internetwork so packets can reach destination networks. Unicast Routing One sender communicates with one intended destination. Routers use forwarding tables populated by static configuration or routing protocols. Distance Vector A router exchanges reachability/distance information with neighbors. The Bellman-Ford family of ideas underlies classic distance-vector routing. RIP is a classic example and uses hop count as its metric, with a maximum usable metric of 15 and 16 rep"},{"title":"Multicast Routing: DVMRP and MOSPF","unit":"Unit 3","url":"units/unit3/lesson07.html","text":" Multicast Routing: DVMRP and MOSPF Learning objectives: Explain multicast routing and outline DVMRP and MOSPF at the syllabus level. Multicast Multicast delivers traffic from one source to a group of receivers. It can reduce duplicate traffic compared with separate unicast streams when many receivers need the same content. DVMRP Distance Vector Multicast Routing Protocol is a multicast routing protocol based on distance-vector ideas and techniques such as reverse-path forwarding/pruning. MOSPF Multicast OSPF extends OSPF concepts for multicast routing by incorporating multicast information into an OSPF-based link-state environment. 2-Mark Answer Multicast routing delivers packets from one s"},{"title":"Lesson 1: Process-to-Process Delivery","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson01.html","text":" Lesson 1: Process-to-Process Delivery Learning objectives: Understand the transport layer's role, process-to-process communication, port numbers, multiplexing and demultiplexing. Introduction The transport layer provides logical communication between application processes running on different hosts. The network layer delivers packets from host to host; the transport layer uses transport addresses, especially port numbers, to deliver data to the correct application endpoint. TCP and UDP are the principal Internet transport protocols discussed in this unit. 0.96 Process-to-Process Communication A process is a running program instance. A host may run many network applications simultaneously. A"},{"title":"Lesson 2: UDP User Datagram Format","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson02.html","text":" Lesson 2: UDP User Datagram Format Learning objectives: Describe UDP, its fixed 8-byte header, every field, characteristics, advantages, limitations and applications. UDP Overview The User Datagram Protocol (UDP) is a connectionless, transaction-oriented transport protocol. RFC 768 specifies a minimal transport mechanism with source and destination ports, length and checksum. It does not provide TCP-style reliable, ordered byte-stream delivery or connection establishment. 0.96 UDP User Datagram Format The UDP header is 8 bytes: The minimum UDP length is 8 bytes because the header itself is 8 bytes. UDP Characteristics Connectionless: no TCP-style handshake before sending data. Message/datag"},{"title":"Lesson 3: UDP Operations and Checksum","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson03.html","text":" Lesson 3: UDP Operations and Checksum Learning objectives: Explain UDP send/receive operations and solve one's-complement checksum problems accurately. UDP Operations UDP communication is simple: The application supplies a message to UDP. UDP identifies the source and destination ports. UDP forms a datagram and calculates the checksum when checksum protection is used. The datagram is handed to IP for transmission. At the receiver, UDP validates the checksum as applicable and uses the destination port to deliver the payload to the correct socket. UDP Checksum RFC 768 defines a 16-bit one's-complement checksum. The checksum calculation conceptually covers a pseudo-header derived from IP infor"},{"title":"Lesson 4: TCP Services","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson04.html","text":" Lesson 4: TCP Services Learning objectives: Explain TCP's connection-oriented service, reliability, byte-stream model, full-duplex communication, flow control and congestion-related behavior. TCP Overview The Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) is an Internet transport protocol that provides a reliable, in-order byte-stream service to applications. RFC 9293 is the current consolidated base TCP specification and replaces RFC 793 as the core specification. Major TCP Services Connection-oriented service: endpoints establish a logical connection before normal data transfer. Reliable delivery: sequence numbers, acknowledgments, checksums and retransmission mechanisms support reliable delivery. O"},{"title":"Lesson 5: TCP Segment Format","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson05.html","text":" Lesson 5: TCP Segment Format Learning objectives: Identify TCP header fields, their sizes and functions, including current TCP control flags. TCP Segment TCP carries application bytes in segments. Each segment has a TCP header followed by optional data. RFC 9293 specifies the header format. 0.98 TCP Header Fields Important Flags SYN: synchronize sequence numbers / initiate connection. ACK: acknowledgment field is valid. FIN: sender has no more data to send and requests orderly close. RST: reset/abort a connection or reject an inappropriate segment. PSH: requests prompt delivery of buffered data to the receiving application in the applicable TCP semantics. URG: urgent pointer is significant."},{"title":"Lesson 6: TCP Connection Establishment and Termination","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson06.html","text":" Lesson 6: TCP Connection Establishment and Termination Learning objectives: Explain the three-way handshake, sequence-number synchronization and four-step orderly termination. Three-Way Handshake TCP uses a three-way exchange to establish a connection and synchronize initial sequence numbers. 0.96 SYN: client sends SYN with initial sequence number $x$. SYN+ACK: server acknowledges $x$ with ACK number $x+1$ and sends its own initial sequence number $y$. ACK: client acknowledges the server's SYN with ACK number $y+1$. After the third exchange, both endpoints can enter the ESTABLISHED state and exchange application data. Why Three Messages? The exchange lets each endpoint learn that the other "},{"title":"Lesson 7: TCP Flow Control","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson07.html","text":" Lesson 7: TCP Flow Control Learning objectives: Explain receiver-based flow control, advertised window, sliding window and the relationship between rwnd and cwnd. Definition Flow control prevents a fast sender from overwhelming a slower receiver. TCP implements receiver flow control using the advertised receive window (rwnd). 0.96 How TCP Flow Control Works The receiver has a finite receive buffer. The receiver advertises available buffer space in the Window field. The sender limits the amount of unacknowledged data so it does not exceed the receiver's advertised capacity. As the application consumes received bytes, the receiver can advertise additional window space. Sliding Window TCP's se"},{"title":"Lesson 8: TCP Error Control","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson08.html","text":" Lesson 8: TCP Error Control Learning objectives: Explain how TCP detects corruption and loss and recovers using checksum, sequence numbers, acknowledgments and retransmission. Purpose TCP error control aims to provide reliable byte-stream delivery despite corruption, loss, duplication or reordering. 0.96 Main Mechanisms Checksum: detects corruption in the TCP segment and protected pseudo-header information. Sequence numbers: identify byte positions and permit the receiver to detect missing or duplicate data. Acknowledgments: indicate cumulative reception progress. Retransmission timer: if an expected acknowledgment does not arrive in time, the sender may retransmit. Duplicate ACKs / fast re"},{"title":"Lesson 9: TCP Congestion Control","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson09.html","text":" Lesson 9: TCP Congestion Control Learning objectives: Understand congestion, cwnd, ssthresh, slow start, congestion avoidance, fast retransmit and fast recovery. Definition Congestion occurs when offered traffic exceeds the capacity available along a path, causing queue buildup, delay and packet loss. TCP congestion control regulates the sender's amount of outstanding data to reduce the risk of overwhelming the network. 0.96 Key Variables cwnd: sender-side congestion window; limits data injected into the network. rwnd: receiver-advertised window; limits data according to receiver capacity. ssthresh: threshold used by the classical algorithm to distinguish slow start from congestion avoidanc"},{"title":"Lesson 10: Quality of Service and Techniques","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson10.html","text":" Lesson 10: Quality of Service and Techniques Learning objectives: Define QoS, explain bandwidth, delay, jitter, packet loss and reliability, and describe common techniques for improving QoS. Definition Quality of Service (QoS) describes the performance characteristics experienced by an application and the mechanisms used to manage those characteristics. Different applications require different service levels. 0.96 QoS Parameters Techniques to Improve QoS Overprovisioning: provide more capacity than average demand. Traffic shaping: regulate the rate or burstiness of traffic, e.g., leaky-bucket or token-bucket concepts. Scheduling/queuing: prioritize or fairly schedule packets using appropria"},{"title":"Lesson 11: Introduction to Network Security","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson11.html","text":" Lesson 11: Introduction to Network Security Learning objectives: Explain security objectives, threats, controls and the CIA triad in a networking context. Definition Network security protects networked information systems, communications and services against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, disruption and misuse. 0.94 CIA Triad Confidentiality: information is not disclosed to unauthorized entities. Integrity: information is not changed in an unauthorized or undetected manner. Availability: authorized users can access systems and information when required. Security Threats Threats may include eavesdropping, tampering, spoofing, replay, malware, denial-of-service, credential the"},{"title":"Lesson 12: Symmetric-Key and Asymmetric Cryptography","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson12.html","text":" Lesson 12: Symmetric-Key and Asymmetric Cryptography Learning objectives: Compare secret-key and public-key cryptography, explain keys, operation, strengths, limitations and typical uses. Cryptography Cryptography uses algorithms and cryptographic keys to protect information. NIST guidance treats cryptographic mechanisms as tools for services such as confidentiality, integrity, authentication and digital signatures. Symmetric-Key Cryptography In symmetric cryptography, communicating parties use the same secret key, or closely related secret key material, for encryption/decryption or other symmetric cryptographic operations. 0.94 Examples of standardized symmetric algorithms include AES-fami"},{"title":"Lesson 13: Message Security","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson13.html","text":" Lesson 13: Message Security Learning objectives: Explain confidentiality, integrity, message digests, MACs and encryption as message-security mechanisms. Meaning Message security protects a message against unauthorized disclosure, modification, forgery or misuse. Different mechanisms provide different services. 0.94 Hash Functions A cryptographic hash function maps an input of arbitrary length to a fixed-length digest. A secure hash is designed so that it is computationally difficult to find collisions and to recover the input from the digest under the appropriate security properties. NIST publications discuss hash functions and their use in digital signatures, HMAC and related applications"},{"title":"Lesson 14: Digital Signature","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson14.html","text":" Lesson 14: Digital Signature Learning objectives: Explain signature generation and verification, security properties, key roles, and the distinction between signatures and encryption. Definition A digital signature is a cryptographic mechanism that allows a verifier to detect unauthorized modification and authenticate the identity associated with the signing key, when the key is properly bound to that identity. NIST FIPS 186-5 specifies algorithms for generating digital signatures and describes their use for integrity and signer authentication. 0.94 Signature Generation A simplified conceptual process is: Compute a cryptographic digest of the message. Use the signer's private key and the st"},{"title":"Lesson 15: User Authentication","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson15.html","text":" Lesson 15: User Authentication Learning objectives: Explain authentication, authenticators, factors, verification and the difference between authentication and authorization. Definition Authentication is the process of establishing that a claimant controls or possesses an authenticator associated with an identity. NIST's current Digital Identity Guidelines, SP 800-63B-4, define requirements for digital authentication and authenticator management. 0.94 Authentication Factors A common educational classification is: Something you know: password, PIN or other memorized secret. Something you have: a hardware token, authenticator device or cryptographic credential. Something you are: a biometric "},{"title":"Lesson 16: E-mail Security","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson16.html","text":" Lesson 16: E-mail Security Learning objectives: Explain e-mail threats and the roles of S/MIME, OpenPGP concepts, encryption and digital signatures. Need for E-mail Security Ordinary e-mail is exposed to threats involving interception, modification, impersonation, malicious attachments, phishing and credential theft. Security mechanisms should address confidentiality, integrity and authentication as required. 0.94 S/MIME S/MIME is a standard for secure MIME data. RFC 8551 describes S/MIME 4.0 and its cryptographic services, including authentication, message integrity and non-repudiation through digital signatures, and confidentiality through encryption. OpenPGP Concept OpenPGP provides mech"},{"title":"Lesson 17: Web Security and Social Issues","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson17.html","text":" Lesson 17: Web Security and Social Issues Learning objectives: Explain HTTPS/TLS at a conceptual level and discuss major social, ethical and human factors in network security. Web Security Web security protects browser-server communication, user accounts, application data and web services. 0.94 HTTPS and TLS HTTPS is HTTP carried over a TLS-protected connection. TLS provides cryptographic mechanisms for confidentiality and integrity and can authenticate the server through certificates and the public-key infrastructure used by the deployment. The exact TLS version and cipher suites are protocol-specific. Common Web Threats Credential theft and phishing. Session theft and insecure session man"},{"title":"Lesson 18: Numerical Problems and Complete Unit Revision","unit":"Unit 4","url":"units/unit4/lesson18.html","text":" Lesson 18: Numerical Problems and Complete Unit Revision Learning objectives: Practice checksum, parity, TCP sequence/ACK, window and congestion calculations and consolidate the complete unit. A. UDP Checksum Practice 0.96 Problem 1 Given 16-bit words 1234, 5678 and 9ABC. Find the checksum. Solution: 1234+5678=68AC \\] 68AC+9ABC=10368 \\] End-around carry: 0368+1=0369 \\] Complement: FC96 \\] Problem 2 Given 1111, 2222, 3333, 4444: 1111+2222+3333+4444=AAAA \\] \\boxed{Checksum=5555} \\] Verification: AAAA+5555=FFFF. \\] Problem 3 Given ABCD, 1234, FFFF: ABCD+1234+FFFF=1BE00 \\] Fold carry: BE00+1=BE01 \\] Complement: 41FE \\] Verification: BE01+41FE=FFFF. \\] B. Even and Odd Parity — Cross-Unit Revisio"},{"title":"Domain Name System (DNS): Distribution of Name Space and DNS in the Internet","unit":"Unit 5","url":"units/unit5/lesson01.html","text":" Domain Name System (DNS): Distribution of Name Space and DNS in the Internet Learning objectives: Explain DNS hierarchy, name resolution, records, caching and Internet operation. 0.92 Purpose DNS maps human-readable domain names to resource records used by network applications. It is a distributed, hierarchical naming system. Name Space The hierarchy has a root, top-level domains and delegated authoritative zones. A domain may contain subdomains and host/service records. Resolution A client normally queries a recursive resolver. If the answer is not cached, the resolver may query authoritative infrastructure through the hierarchy and return the result to the client. Common Records A maps a "},{"title":"Electronic Mail: Architecture and Services","unit":"Unit 5","url":"units/unit5/lesson02.html","text":" Electronic Mail: Architecture and Services Learning objectives: Explain e-mail architecture, user agents, mail servers and basic message delivery. 0.9 Architecture Electronic mail involves user agents, mail submission/transfer components, mailboxes and retrieval/access mechanisms. SMTP is used for message transfer/submission; POP3 is one of the protocols used to retrieve mail. Services Typical services include composing, addressing, submission, transfer, storage and retrieval. Message Path A sender's mail user agent submits a message to a mail service. Mail transfer systems use SMTP to relay it toward the recipient's mail system, where it is stored for later access by the recipient. 2-Mark "},{"title":"POP3 and SMTP","unit":"Unit 5","url":"units/unit5/lesson03.html","text":" POP3 and SMTP Learning objectives: Explain the basic functions and distinction between POP3 and SMTP. SMTP Simple Mail Transfer Protocol is an application-layer protocol used to transfer mail between mail systems and for message submission in appropriate deployments. POP3 Post Office Protocol version 3 provides a simple mechanism for a client to retrieve messages from a maildrop. It commonly uses a download-oriented model; modern deployments may provide server-side synchronization through other protocols such as IMAP, but IMAP is outside this syllabus. 2-Mark Answer SMTP sends/transfers e-mail; POP3 retrieves e-mail from a maildrop. 5-Mark Answer Differentiate SMTP and POP3. 10-Mark Answer "},{"title":"File Transfer Protocol (FTP)","unit":"Unit 5","url":"units/unit5/lesson04.html","text":" File Transfer Protocol (FTP) Learning objectives: Explain FTP architecture, control/data concepts and basic operations. 0.9 Purpose FTP is an application-layer protocol for transferring files between a client and server. Classic FTP separates control communication from data transfer. Basic Operations Users authenticate as required, navigate directories, upload/download files and terminate the session. Commands are carried over the control connection; file/listing transfers use the data connection. Security Note Classic FTP does not by itself provide modern cryptographic confidentiality. Secure file-transfer deployments commonly use other mechanisms, but the syllabus topic here is FTP itself"},{"title":"World Wide Web and HTTP","unit":"Unit 5","url":"units/unit5/lesson05.html","text":" World Wide Web and HTTP Learning objectives: Explain the WWW, client-server model, HTTP request/response and basic methods/status concepts. 0.9 WWW The World Wide Web is a distributed hypermedia system in which clients retrieve resources from web servers using application protocols such as HTTP. HTTP HTTP is a request/response application protocol. A client sends a request identifying a resource and desired operation; the server returns a response containing a status code and, when applicable, a representation of the resource. Common Methods GET retrieves a representation; POST submits data for processing; PUT replaces/creates a representation at a target resource when supported; DELETE req"}];